---
description: Call constructors and library functions directly; no thin wrapper/alias helpers
alwaysApply: true
---

# No thin wrapper functions

Don't add helpers whose only job is to forward arguments to an existing class or
function with a few of them pre-filled or renamed (e.g. a `_linear(...)` that just
builds a `LinearDecode`). Call the real constructor/function at each site, even if
the calls look repetitive. The reader should see the actual API being used.

```python
# ❌ BAD
def _linear(weights, name, device, cache, dtype, rectangle_b_grid=False):
    return LinearDecode(weights[f"{name}.weight"], device, cache.file(name), dtype=dtype,
                        **DECODE_LAYOUTS[name], rectangle_b_grid=rectangle_b_grid, use_rm_hs=True)

self.comp_kv_proj = _linear(weights, "compressor.wkv", device, cache, ttnn.bfloat16)

# ✅ GOOD
self.comp_kv_proj = LinearDecode(
    weights["compressor.wkv.weight"],
    device,
    cache.file("compressor.wkv"),
    dtype=ttnn.bfloat16,
    **DECODE_LAYOUTS["compressor.wkv"],
    use_rm_hs=True,
)
```

A helper is fine when it adds real logic (math, branching, a non-trivial
transformation), not just argument plumbing.
